  "metabolism": "Evidence is from Palladone 1.3 mg capsules (oral). Hydromorphone is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and undergoes pre-systemic elimination, giving an oral bioavailability of about 32%. Plasma protein binding is low (<10%). It is metabolised by direct conjugation or reduction of the keto group with subsequent conjugation, primarily to hydromorphone-3-glucuronide, hydromorphone-3-glucoside and dihydroisomorphine-6-glucuronide; smaller amounts of dihydroisomorphine-6-glucoside, dihydromorphine and dihydroisomorphine were also found. It is metabolised in the liver, with a smaller portion excreted unchanged via the kidneys. The label reports no indication of metabolism in vivo via the cytochrome P450 system; in vitro hydromorphone has only minor inhibition of CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C8, 2D6 and 3A4 (IC50 > 50 micromolar).",  "metabolismSources": ["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/7686/smpc"]
